\f1\fs22 The Johnny strode through the Resonance Cluster of Project Kebab\'92s research facility, deep beneath Ruined Castle, his confidence having grown in proportion to the distance he maintained from Sarah. The three Sages\'97Kleo, Solo, and Solo\'97trailed behind him, their robes incongruous among the sterile lab equipment and holographic displays.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip Stockwell lingered near the back of the group, trying to appear inconspicuous despite his new Shakti Enterprises lab coat. The former Stasis Anomalies Investigator kept nervously adjusting his security badge, acutely wondering if his former employers knew where he was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93As you can see, gentlemen,\'94 The Johnny said with the practiced charm of someone who\'92d spent a lifetime taking credit for others\'92 work, \'93we\'92ve made significant progress in decoding the Gyges Disc\'92s properties. If you\'92ll observe these readings...\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He gestured expansively toward a holographic display where patterns of concentric circles pulsed and shifted, their edges blurring and reforming in hypnotic rhythm. Dr. Quark, one of the senior physics researchers, stood nearby, his posture stiff with the awkwardness of a scientist suddenly required to perform for visitors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip\'92s eyes widened at the display. The patterns matched classified Fridge Force files he\'92d only glimpsed during an incident briefing\'97one where three senior agents had suffered unexplained seizures just from viewing similar recordings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Fascinating,\'94 Solo murmured, stepping closer to the display than protocol would normally allow. Unlike his fellow Sages, whose attention remained politely distant, Solo\'92s eyes locked onto the patterns with intensity. \'94And you believe these are messages?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not believe\'97know,\'94 Johnny corrected, enjoying the rare opportunity to correct someone else. \'94Dr. Quark has confirmed that each pattern corresponds to universes with different universal gravitational constants.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo nodded slowly, as if absorbing this information for the first time. \'93Have you attempted to adjust your instruments to compensate for temporal variance as well as gravitational?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr. Quark frowned. \'94Temporal variance?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s extremely dangerous,\'94 Chip blurted out before he could stop himself. All eyes turned to him, and he immediately regretted speaking. \'93I mean... Fridge Force has... that is... stasis technology operates on similar principles, and temporal calibration has been known to cause... incidents.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nonsense,\'94 Johnny said. After a moment of reflection while everyone stared at him, he raised an eyebrow.  \'93What kind of incidents?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip swallowed hard. \'93Classified.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A mere suggestion,\'94 Chilo said smoothly, his voice dropping to a casual murmur. \'93If gravity differs between universes, surely time flows differently as well. Perhaps a matrix calibration accounting for relative chronological displacement might... but I\'92m no physicist.\'94 He waved his hand dismissively at his own suggestion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And what did Fridge Force regulations say about such calibrations, Mr. Stockwell?\'94 Kleo asked innocently.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip felt trapped between his new obligation to Shakti Enterprises and the dangers of crossing Fridge Force. \'93The Food Preservation Secrets Act prohibits me from discussing specific technical details, but...\'94 he lowered his voice, \'93there\'92s a reason stasis fields are locally contained and never allowed to resonate with one another.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny\'92s eyes narrowed. He glanced at Dr. Quark, whose expression had shifted from confusion to consideration.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s an interesting thought,\'94 Dr. Quark admitted. \'93We could modify the phase variance compensator to include a temporal coefficient, with appropriate safety measures.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94Precisely what I was about to suggest,\'94 Johnny said quickly. \'94Let\'92s implement that immediately.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I strongly advise against this,\'94 Chip said, his face pale. \'93At minimum, we should conduct a full containment assessment before\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mr. Stockwell,\'94 Johnny interrupted, \'93your expertise in fresh food is noted, but this is cutting-edge physics. Dr. Quark, proceed with the modifications.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the scientists turned their attention to the equipment, Solo drew close to Chilo. \'94What are you doing?\'94 he whispered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Accelerating inevitability,\'94 Chilo replied, his voice barely audible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94This isn\'92t what we discussed,\'94 Solo hissed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo merely smiled, watching as Johnny began directing lab technicians to modify their instruments according to calculations he clearly didn\'92t understand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip stood frozen in place, internal alarms blaring. He\'92d seen the classified reports on \'93temporal resonance events.\'94 But revealing that would mean admitting to knowledge he wasn\'92t supposed to have. Instead, he quietly slipped away, heading for his office to compose an urgent message to Sarah Jacobson.
